{"id": "aic-12947", "status": "ok", "meta": "Nicolaes Berchem's 1680 etching Cow Drinking, where the cow is not drinking, made from a 1679 drawing now in Paris.", "about": "The cow in this print is not drinking. Plenty else is going on, though. Nicolaes Berchem was the most famous Dutch painter of idyllic Italian views, and he also etched more than 50 pastoral scenes of shepherds and their animals. The museum calls this a mature and ambitious print. Berchem made it from a drawing of 1679 that is now in the Dutuit Collection in Paris.", "museum_record": "https://www.artic.edu/artworks/12947", "inventory_number": "", "dimensions": "Plate: 27.7 × 37.4 cm (10 15/16 × 14 3/4 in.); Sheet: 28.1 × 37.8 cm (11 1/8 × 14 15/16 in.)", "credit_line": "", "sources": ["https://www.artic.edu/artworks/12947", "https://artnap.com/artwork/aic-12947"], "check": "clean"}
{"id": "aic-16434", "status": "ok", "meta": "The Virgin and Child appear on an altar in golden clouds with Saints Dominic and Hyacinth, Giovanni Battista Tiepolo's altarpiece of 1730 to 1735.", "about": "The infant Christ hands Saint Dominic a rosary. Tiepolo shows the Virgin and Child as if they had just appeared on an altar in a light-filled vision, among golden clouds. Dominic founded a preaching order closely tied to praying with the rosary. Scenes embroidered on the altar cloth show the mysteries of the rosary, key events in the lives of Mary and Jesus. A believer would have thought about those images while working through the prayers marked by the beads.", "museum_record": "https://www.artic.edu/artworks/16434", "inventory_number": "", "dimensions": "274.3 × 137.2 cm (108 × 54 in.); Framed: 303.6 × 174 × 12.7 cm (119 1/2 × 68 1/2 × 5 in.)", "credit_line": "", "sources": ["https://www.artic.edu/artworks/16434", "https://artnap.com/artwork/aic-16434"], "check": "clean"}
{"id": "aic-23506", "status": "ok", "meta": "A young steer grazing in an unseen field, one of Albrecht Dürer's earliest animal drawings, made around 1496.", "about": "A young steer grazes in a field we cannot see. The museum counts it among Dürer's earliest animal drawings. Sure strokes and careful cross-hatching give the animal a solid shape, with tight skin over a strong but bony frame. Sketchy lines near the right hind foot, the back, the muzzle and the horns show that Dürer first drew the steer in a slightly different pose, with its mouth open. The museum thinks it was probably a study from nature. He later reused the hindquarters in his engraving The Prodigal Son amid the Swine.", "museum_record": "https://www.artic.edu/artworks/23506", "inventory_number": "", "dimensions": "17.5 × 14 cm (6 15/16 × 5 9/16 in.)", "credit_line": "", "sources": ["https://www.artic.edu/artworks/23506", "https://artnap.com/artwork/aic-23506"], "check": "clean"}
{"id": "aic-49387", "status": "ok", "meta": "A grinning skeleton broadside about the Mexican Revolution: José Guadalupe Posada's Revolutionary Calavera, made around 1910.", "about": "Skeletons celebrate the destructive power of the Mexican Revolution on this Day of the Dead broadside. José Guadalupe Posada made thousands of cheap, colorful handbills for working and middle class city readers. They covered news, scandals and oddities. The museum says his reputation as an artist loyal to Mexican traditions and popular causes rests largely on sheets like this one. The calavera, a satirical skeleton, is the figure he made popular. Later, the Taller de Gráfica Popular borrowed its humor and manic energy and named Posada as their forebear.", "museum_record": "https://www.artic.edu/artworks/49387", "inventory_number": "", "dimensions": "40 × 30 cm (15 3/4 × 11 13/16 in.)", "credit_line": "", "sources": ["https://www.artic.edu/artworks/49387", "https://artnap.com/artwork/aic-49387"], "check": "clean"}
{"id": "cma-142696", "status": "ok", "meta": "A vaporous human form drifts above in Odilon Redon's 1896 lithograph, from a series inspired by Bulwer-Lytton's novel The Haunted and the Haunters.", "about": "This lithograph belongs to a series Odilon Redon made after The Haunted and the Haunters, a ghost story by the British writer Edward Bulwer-Lytton. Redon read a great deal of new literature, and the museum says the prints catch the book's mysterious tone with vague images that sometimes verge on abstraction. He worked with the master printer Auguste Clot. The soft range of tones came from turning the lithographic crayon on its side, layering marks, and scratching straight into the stone.", "museum_record": "https://clevelandart.org/art/1966.413.2", "inventory_number": "", "dimensions": "", "credit_line": "", "sources": ["https://clevelandart.org/art/1966.413.2", "https://artnap.com/artwork/cma-142696"], "check": "clean"}
{"id": "si-saam_1977.101.1", "status": "ok", "meta": "Merchant Jeremiah Williams of Roxbury, Massachusetts, in a stern pose with papers and books, painted by Christian Gullager.", "about": "Jeremiah Williams sold West India goods in Roxbury, Massachusetts. In 1787 he married Matilda Davis, whose father Caleb Davis was an important man in Boston. His obituary mentioned that wealthy father-in-law, which suggests the marriage lifted his own standing. Christian Gullager painted him in handsome clothes with a stern, imposing look. Papers in his hand and books on the table behind often appeared in colonial portraits to show education and refinement. The museum says the portrait shows a social rank men like Williams hoped for, even if they had not reached it.", "museum_record": "https://americanart.si.edu/collections/search/artwork/?id=9733", "inventory_number": "", "dimensions": "", "credit_line": "", "sources": ["https://americanart.si.edu/collections/search/artwork/?id=9733", "https://artnap.com/artwork/si-saam_1977.101.1"], "check": "clean"}
